  <section xml:id="sec-more-help-resources">
    <title>More R-Instat Help and Resources</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-60">
      <caption>Labels/Levels dialog</caption>
      <image source="images/ch1/image14.png" width="45%">
        <description><p>Labels/Levels dialog</p></description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>The Help menu gives access to broader sections of the internal help file, including a statistical glossary and the R-Instat Resources site.</p>

    <p>The Resources site is an online webpage with a range of tutorials for learning more about R-Instat and good statistical practice. Many activities include both videos and written follow-along documents, so you can choose the format that works best for you.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-multipage-dataset">
    <title>A Multipage Dataset</title>

    <p>We believe that using a broad range of real data is central to good statistics teaching, and R-Instat connects you to many options.</p>

    <p>
      Fig. 60, shows the Data section of the help file.
      This outlines the different sources of data that are available through R-Instat.
      Many datasets are included within the program and can be accessed through the library.
      However, R-Instat also points you towards larger external datasets when you are ready to work with more complex examples.
    </p>

    <p>One option is UNICEF&#8217;s Multiple Indicator Cluster Surveys. The help file links to guidance on accessing these data, including a video tutorial. Here, we briefly show an example using data from Pakistan.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch1-61">
      <caption>MICS Data Set</caption>
      <image source="images/ch1/image33.png" width="80%">
        <description><p>MICS Data Set</p></description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>You can see that this is a large dataset. The page (or sheet) in Fig. 61 has 418 columns and 42,408 rows, and the full databook contains eight different sheets.</p>

    <p>
      This highlights a major advantage of R-Instat&#8217;s databook system: multiple related data frames can be kept together in one databook.
      This makes it possible to explore a study at different levels.
      In this example, some pages contain household-level information, while others contain individual-level information for members of those households.
      All the pages of a databook remain connected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-reflections">
    <title>Reflections</title>

    <p>
      This chapter has introduced more than a set of R-Instat tools.
      It has introduced a way of working with real data.
      The central habit should be asking: <em>What is this data telling me, and does it make sense?</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      That is why we have called this data-detective work.
      Real data rarely arrives perfectly ready for analysis.
      Column types may need changing, factor levels may reveal unexpected patterns, labels may be unclear, and some variables may need cleaning before they can be used with confidence.
      These are not distractions from statistics. They are part of doing statistics well.
    </p>

    <p>
      Factors are central to that process.
      They show how data can be organised into meaningful groups.
      They make the data easier to summarise, compare, reorder, filter, and subset.
      Factors also help expose problems.
      Extra or unexpected levels may reveal typing errors, inconsistent responses, or hidden structure in the data.
    </p>

    <p>Data cleaning and data analysis are closely connected. Cleaning is not a chore to complete before the &#8220;real&#8221; statistics begins. It is often through cleaning that you understand the data better.</p>

    <p>
      The wider message is that good statistical work depends on curiosity and care.
      Real datasets are rarely perfect, and they often need to be explored from more than one angle.
      R-Instat is designed to support that process: it helps you look at the data, notice problems, make improvements, and keep track of the different versions or subsets you create.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is more to learn, and you do not need to learn it all at once.
      The R-Instat Help system, <url href="https://ecampus.r-instat.org/mod/page/view.php?id=212">Moodle</url> site (including: introductory tutorials and videos) provide further guidance when you want more detail.
      The aim of this chapter has been to give you a practical starting point and start building confidence in your ability to handle the data problems you meet.
    </p>
  </section>

